      "adaptations": "Foundation: walk, soft ball, wide target, no pressure. Intermediate: light pace and one decision. Advanced: narrow target, short time, or weak hand. Large group: six zones. Small group: 2v2. Limited space: walking play. Partial equipment: one ball per pair. Varied needs: visual cue, high-contrast mark, extra time, consistent partner, adapted range.",
      "assessment": "Checks: performance against each of the six learning objectives in section 3 — stance, elbow, weight transfer and follow-through, hitting the target, hitting the coloured late-cue target, and the open-zone choice. Success: at least 5 of the 6 listed objectives achieved, each per its own stated measure. Partial: 3–4 objectives achieved. Not yet: 0–2 objectives, or unsafe action; return to slow, unopposed practice.",
      "reflection": "Teacher: Who acted independently? Which cue worked? Was movement time equal? Students: What did you see before acting? What will you change? Next lesson: select one group criterion to revisit.",
      "continuity": "If met: HB-05 — three-step rhythm and finish. If not: return to the unopposed version, enlarge the target, and practise at walking pace.",
      "bankLink": "Tags: handball, overarm throwing, school. Age: 10–12. Equipment: balls, cones, bibs. Skills: throwing, weight transfer, accuracy, force control. Load: moderate. Complexity: intermediate. Keywords: overarm throw, side-on stance, high elbow, weight transfer, follow-through, target accuracy, force control, cone goal.",
      "systemData": "Physical load: moderate. Cognitive load: moderate–high. Organizational load: moderate. Student independence: medium.",
      "teacherSummary": "State the goal and success cues.\nWarm up through movement, scanning, and paired passing.\nBuild overarm throwing first without pressure.\nAdd a decision and a no-contact small-sided game.\nClose with evidence of success and a next step.",
      "pedagogicalValue": "Why important: It builds accuracy and a safe shoulder action before speed, movement, and jumping are layered on — the order that protects a growing arm. Develops: the overarm throwing pattern with a high elbow, weight transfer from back foot to front, force calibration to distance, and target selection under a late cue. Why not skip: students left to invent a throw push the ball from the chest with a square body, which is inaccurate, produces no range, and loads the shoulder badly once they begin throwing hard.",
