      "adaptations": "Foundation: walk, soft ball, wide target, no pressure. Intermediate: light pace and one decision. Advanced: narrow target, short time, or weak hand. Large group: six zones. Small group: 2v2. Limited space: walking play. Partial equipment: one ball per pair. Varied needs: visual cue, high-contrast mark, extra time, consistent partner, adapted range.",
      "assessment": "Checks: technique, gaze, decision, spacing, safety. Success: 4–5 criteria in most rounds. Partial: 2–3 with prompting. Not yet: 0–1 or unsafe action; return to slow, unopposed practice.",
      "reflection": "Teacher: Who acted independently? Which cue worked? Was movement time equal? Students: What did you see before acting? What will you change? Next lesson: select one group criterion to revisit.",
      "continuity": "If met: HB-06 — no-contact defence and lane control. If not: return to the unopposed version, enlarge the target, and practise at walking pace.",
      "bankLink": "Tags: handball, three-step finish, school. Age: 12–14. Equipment: balls, cones, bibs. Skills: step rhythm, catch, throw, balance. Load: moderate. Complexity: intermediate. Keywords: three-step rhythm, catch in stride, step count, balanced finish, moving throw, travelling rule, timing the reception.",
      "systemData": "Physical load: moderate. Cognitive load: moderate–high. Organizational load: moderate. Student independence: medium.",
      "teacherSummary": "State the goal and success cues.\nWarm up through movement, scanning, and paired passing.\nBuild three-step finish first without pressure.\nAdd a decision and a no-contact small-sided game.\nClose with evidence of success and a next step.",
      "pedagogicalValue": "Why important: It connects static technique to a moving finish, and the three-step rule is the one law of handball that decides whether an attack is legal at all. Develops: step rhythm and the timing of a reception in stride, balance on a moving take-off, judgement of how many steps a distance allows, and the ability to hold throwing form while the legs are working. Why not skip: students who never rehearse the count either travel constantly, which kills every attack they build, or stop dead before throwing, which hands the defence all the time it needs.",
