      "bankLink": "Tags: handball, no-contact defence, school. Age: 12–14. Equipment: balls, cones, bibs. Skills: defensive stance, lateral movement, distance, communication. Load: moderate. Complexity: intermediate. Keywords: defensive stance, lateral side steps, no-contact defence, defensive distance, steering the attacker, lane control, delay, recovery.",
      "systemData": "Physical load: moderate. Cognitive load: moderate–high. Organizational load: moderate. Student independence: medium.",
      "teacherSummary": "State the goal and success cues.\nWarm up through movement, scanning, and paired passing.\nBuild no-contact defence first without pressure.\nAdd a decision and a no-contact small-sided game.\nClose with evidence of success and a next step.",
      "pedagogicalValue": "Why important: Handball tolerates contact at senior level, so a school class needs a positional model of defending taught explicitly — it is what keeps competitive play safe and fair rather than physical. Develops: a low balanced stance, lateral footwork without crossing, judgement of a distance that delays without inviting contact, and the communication that stops two defenders marking one attacker. Why not skip: without it students defend by grabbing shirts and blocking with the body, producing the injuries and arguments that lead some teachers to drop handball from the programme altogether.",
