      "sections": {
        "opening": "3 minutes. Teacher says: “Today I am not counting goals. I am watching what you choose, how you move after you pass, and how fast you get back — and I will tell you exactly what I saw.” Organisation: class in a half-circle with the five criteria displayed or read aloud. Purpose: make the assessment criteria transparent before any play begins, so nobody is judged against a standard they did not know.",
        "warmup": "7 minutes. Purpose: prepare the whole body for even-sided play and briefly revisit each pathway skill, so the assessment is fair to students who need a reminder. Structure: individual movement, then fours with a ball. Instructions: jog with direction changes on a colour call and add arm circles and side steps; then in fours pass and move through four cones, changing the condition each minute — first pass and move, then add a three-step finish, then add an immediate role change on a signal. Teaching points: this is rehearsal, not yet assessment; ask now if a cue is unclear. Transitions: move to game courts by colour. Variations: foundation — walk and use a soft ball; advanced — add a shadow defender.",
        "mainA": "10 minutes — goal: refresh the five pathway skills\nProcedure: Five brief stations — dribble and stop, support after passing, overarm throw to target, defensive stance and steering, and transition on a signal. Each student picks one personal focus to sharpen before the assessed games. Work 40 seconds, then 20 seconds of feedback, in four groups.\nOrganisation: five marked stations with one rotation on the teacher's signal; nothing is recorded at this stage, so students can still make mistakes freely.\nTeaching points: Choose the station that is genuinely weakest rather than the most comfortable; one correction per student, not a list.\nCommon errors: treating the stations as a competition; practising what is already secure; rushing the rotation and arriving without having worked.",
        "mainB": "10 minutes — goal: apply advantage and support in 4v3\nProcedure: Four attackers against three defenders in 75-second rounds, observed against the five criteria. An observer records what is seen — behaviour only, never a personal judgement — and roles change every minute so everyone is observed both attacking and defending.\nOrganisation: groups of seven or eight with two rotating observers holding a simple criteria card; the teacher moves between courts, observing one student for 45–60 seconds at a time.\nTeaching points: Use the extra player rather than forcing the ball; defenders protect the centre and talk; play at a pace at which the choice is visible.\nCommon errors: the strongest student taking every attack; observers writing a verdict instead of a behaviour; defenders reverting to contact once the score matters; quiet play with no communication.",
        "appliedGame": "10 minutes. Purpose: provide the fairest evidence available — even-sided continuous play in which nothing is cued and every student decides independently. Rules: 4v4, no contact, no goalkeeper. One point for a goal, one bonus point when every team member touched the ball during the attack, and one bonus point for restoring defensive shape within three seconds of losing possession. Three seconds maximum while holding. Teaching points: the assessment is separate from the score, so a losing team can still show the better play; include the quieter players deliberately. Common errors: chasing goals and abandoning the criteria; excluding a weaker team-mate late in the game; contact returning under pressure.\nScoring: one point for successful execution and a bonus for the correct decision; no individual ranking.\nVariation: wider space for foundation; time limit for advanced learners.",
        "closing": "5 minutes. Key message: “What I write down about you today is a decision you made, not a goal you scored — so tell me the best decision you made, and the one thing you want to fix.” Learning summary: each student records one strength and one target, supported by something they or their observer actually saw. Feedback: teacher gives brief individual feedback and links the evidence to the next unit; no public ranking of any kind. Orderly exit: colour-coded collection and a head count."
      },
