      "adaptations": "Foundation: walk, soft ball, wide target, no pressure. Intermediate: light pace and one decision. Advanced: narrow target, short time, or weak hand. Large group: six zones. Small group: 2v2. Limited space: walking play. Partial equipment: one ball per pair. Varied needs: visual cue, high-contrast mark, extra time, consistent partner, adapted range.",
      "assessment": "Checks: five criteria — transition to defence within three seconds of losing possession, an appropriate decision in 3 of 4 situations, combining at least 3 of the 5 pathway skills in live play, contact-free defence at arm's-length distance, and active, effective participation across every role, evidenced by what was actually observed rather than the score. Success: at least 4 of the 5 criteria achieved, each judged by its own measure. Partial: 2–3 criteria achieved. Not yet: 0–1 criterion, or unsafe action; return to slow, unopposed practice.",
      "reflection": "Teacher: Who acted independently? Which cue worked? Was movement time equal? Students: What did you see before acting? What will you change? Next lesson: select one group criterion to revisit.",
      "continuity": "If met: HB-09 — width, depth, and line support in 4v4. If not: return to the unopposed version, enlarge the target, and practise at walking pace.",
      "bankLink": "Tags: handball, final applied game, school. Age: 13–16. Equipment: balls, cones, bibs. Skills: decisions, passing, finishing, defence, transition. Load: moderate. Complexity: intermediate. Keywords: authentic assessment, process criteria, observer card, even-sided game, transfer of learning, inclusion, personal target, no-contact play.",
      "systemData": "Physical load: moderate. Cognitive load: moderate–high. Organizational load: moderate. Student independence: medium.",
      "teacherSummary": "State the goal and success cues.\nWarm up through movement, scanning, and paired passing.\nBuild final applied game first without pressure.\nAdd a decision and a no-contact small-sided game.\nClose with evidence of success and a next step.",
      "pedagogicalValue": "Why important: It is the only lesson that shows which skills actually transfer from closed practice into live play, and it does so against criteria students were told in advance rather than a private impression. Develops: independent decision-making without cues, integration of all five pathway skills under fatigue, self-assessment against public standards, and the responsibility to include every team-mate when a result is at stake. Why not skip: a pathway that ends at the last drill leaves both teacher and student guessing; grading on goals alone rewards whoever already plays at a club, and the students who progressed most are precisely the ones who would go unrecognised.",
